  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ボタンウキクサ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%9C%E3%82%BF%E3%83%B3%E3%82%A6%E3%82%AD%E3%82%AF%E3%82%B5",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 109793112,
      "text": "ボタンウキクサ は、単子葉植物サトイモ科に属する水面に浮かぶ熱帯性の水草である。熱帯地方に広く分布し、原産地は南アメリカとも中央アフリカとも言われるが、詳しい原産地は不明。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Pistia」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pistia",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1375227535,
      "text": "Pistia is a genus of aquatic plants in the arum family, Araceae. It is the sole genus in the tribe Pistieae which reflects its systematic isolation within the family. The single species it comprises, Pistia stratiotes, is often called water cabbage, water lettuce, Nile cabbage, or shellflower. Its native distribution is uncertain but is probably pantropical; it was first scientifically described from plants found on the Nile near Lake Victoria in Africa. It is now present, either naturally or through human introduction, in nearly all tropical and subtropical fresh waterways and is considered an invasive species as well as a mosquito breeding habitat. The specific epithet is derived from a Greek word, στρατιώτης, meaning \"soldier\", which references the sword-shaped leaves of some plants in the genus Stratiotes."
    }
  },
